\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuned Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models can achieve high task success yet remain behaviorally unsafe, with collision avoidance rates lagging task completion on safety-critical benchmarks.
We present a factorial study of twelve conditions varying five factors (gradient flow, label content, normalization type, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent) to isolate how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance.
Our central finding is a \textbf{crossover interaction between gradient flow and normalization type}, not a universal effect of gradient flow. Under Batch Normalization (BN), gradient flow degrades safety: Full OCC BN achieves a 0.725 Collision Avoidance Rate versus 0.767 for Detach BN (Cohen's $d = -0.94$, large). Under Layer Normalization (LN), the pattern reverses: Full OCC LN reaches 0.777, above the no-auxiliary-head Vanilla baseline of 0.745 (Cohen's $d = +1.11$, large). The gradient flow main effect is non-significant ($p = 0.396$), the classic signature of a crossover interaction. The BN + gradient flow cell is both the worst-performing and most unstable configuration ($\sigma{=}0.064$, ${>}2.5{\times}$ other cells). Spatial label content (ground-truth occupancy, random binary labels, egocentric depth maps) shows no detectable effect on safety; the gradient pathway, not the spatial information, determines the outcome.
We attribute this interaction to BN's running statistics: gradient flow through auxiliary heads shifts batch statistics in the shared encoder, a coupling absent in LN's per-token computation. Practical guidelines: detach auxiliary gradients under BN architectures; gradient flow is safe under LN; never combine BN with gradient flow.
\end{abstract}

